WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.895
Some configuration values directly change security behavior.

2
00:00:03.895 --> 00:00:20.967
Debug mode, detailed errors, permissive CORS, relaxed authentication, disabled authorization checks, cookie settings, TLS assumptions, broad logging, health detail, test integrations, and experimental features can all change the effective risk of the application.

3
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:26.095
Feature flags are operational controls, but they still require security review.

4
00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:34.145
A flag can change who sees a feature, which integration is used, whether a workflow is enforced, or how much data is logged.

5
00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:40.117
Treat security-relevant flags as controlled configuration, not as harmless product switches.

6
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:43.557
Risky toggles need ownership, expiration, and review.

7
00:00:43.557 --> 00:00:56.475
If a temporary setting is enabled to respond to an incident or unblock testing, the team should know who approved it, why it exists, when it expires, and what must happen before it becomes permanent.

8
00:00:56.475 --> 00:01:00.500
Otherwise emergency settings quietly become long-term posture.

9
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.693
Safe defaults are especially important for new environments and new deployments.

10
00:01:05.693 --> 00:01:16.014
A freshly created staging slot, container environment, or cloud app should not begin life with permissive settings simply because no one has configured it yet.

11
00:01:16.014 --> 00:01:21.142
Defaults should fail closed or choose conservative behavior whenever practical.

12
00:01:21.142 --> 00:01:26.076
Avoid configuration that silently weakens security under failure conditions.

13
00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:37.955
If an identity provider is unavailable, an authorization service cannot be reached, or a feature flag provider times out, the application should not automatically enable broad access.

14
00:01:37.955 --> 00:01:41.135
Failure behavior should be explicit and reviewed.

15
00:01:41.135 --> 00:01:44.836
Document approved values for security-sensitive settings.

16
00:01:44.836 --> 00:01:55.546
Developers and operators should know which values are allowed, which are development-only, which require production approval, and which should trigger investigation.

17
00:01:55.546 --> 00:01:58.467
Clear allowed states reduce accidental drift.
